## Project Overview

MaintiQ is an academic decision-support prototype that predicts whether an industrial machine will fail, based on six operational sensor readings. It provides a native Windows desktop application (PySide6) for importing a CSV dataset, training and comparing classification models, running single-record failure predictions, generating SHAP/LIME explanations, and exporting evaluation reports.

The system addresses predictive maintenance: using operational data to anticipate equipment failure before it occurs, rather than reacting after breakdown or servicing on a fixed schedule. Machine-learning failure prediction learns patterns from labelled historical data (failure / no-failure) and scores new machine states. Explainable AI (SHAP, LIME) makes individual predictions interpretable by attributing the score to contributing features, supporting human review rather than replacing it.

MaintiQ is intended for technical readers, university project evaluation, and software-engineering portfolio use. It is not a certified industrial diagnostic system.

---

## Problem Statement

Industrial maintenance commonly follows two strategies:

- **Reactive maintenance** — repair after failure. Unplanned downtime, emergency parts, and production loss are typical costs.
- **Time-based preventive maintenance** — service on a fixed calendar interval regardless of actual machine condition. Components are replaced before end-of-life, wasting usable capacity, and failures can still occur between intervals.

A data-driven approach uses operational telemetry (temperature, speed, torque, tool wear, machine type) to predict binary machine failure (`0` = no failure, `1` = failure) for a given machine state. MaintiQ demonstrates this approach end-to-end: dataset import and validation, preprocessing, feature engineering, model training and comparison, threshold analysis, prediction, explainability, and reporting — all within a desktop application.

**Academic prototype distinction.** MaintiQ is a prototype built on the public AI4I 2020 benchmark dataset. Predictions and explanations are model outputs, not certified industrial diagnoses or maintenance instructions. Qualified human review is required before acting on any output. The application does not connect to machinery, PLCs, SCADA, or MES systems.

---

## Project Objectives

The implemented objectives are:

1. **Dataset inspection and validation** — load and validate an AI4I-style CSV against a formal schema; reject missing required columns, invalid target values, unexpected categories, and out-of-domain numerics.
2. **Data preprocessing** — leakage-safe imputation, categorical encoding, and scaling fitted on the training partition only; stratified train/test split.
3. **Feature engineering** — derive `temperature_diff`, `power_kw`, `wear_rate_proxy`, `torque_normalized`, and `temp_wear_interaction` from the six canonical inputs.
4. **Classification model training** — train Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and XGBoost with reproducible random seeds and class-imbalance handling.
5. **Model evaluation and comparison** — compute accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC, confusion matrix, specificity, balanced accuracy, FPR, FNR, and average precision on a held-out test set; compare all four models on identical partitions.
6. **Threshold analysis** — leakage-safe threshold selection on validation scores only, with deterministic tie-breaking.
7. **Failure prediction** — single-record inference through a saved pipeline, with out-of-distribution warnings.
8. **Explainable AI** — local SHAP and LIME explanations, plus global SHAP feature importance where the backend is available.
9. **Reporting** — export metrics, dataset profiles, threshold analysis, and evaluation charts to JSON, CSV, and PNG.
10. **Windows desktop application delivery** — native PySide6 GUI with 12 pages, background workers for long-running tasks, and a PyInstaller portable executable.

---

## Key Features

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Desktop dashboard | Status summary (dataset, registered models, selected model, last training) with quick navigation to import, train, predict, and reports. |
| Dataset import and validation | CSV import via file dialog or menu; schema validation against the AI4I contract; original source file is never modified. |
| Dataset exploration | View dataset profile and active-frame metadata. |
| Exploratory data analysis | Visualisation page for dataset inspection. |
| Model training | Train one of four candidate classifiers on the active dataset; background worker with progress reporting and safe cancellation. |
| Prediction | Single-record failure prediction through a registered model, with class, score, threshold, and out-of-distribution warnings. |
| Model comparison | Train and compare all four candidates on identical partitions; report per-model status (success/unavailable/failed). |
| Performance evaluation | Metrics, confusion matrix, ROC and precision-recall charts on the held-out test set. |
| Model registry | JSON-backed registry with monotonic versioning (v1, v2, …), per-artefact SHA-256 hashes verified before load, atomic writes, and delete UX. |
| SHAP explanations | Local `KernelExplainer` explanations and global mean-absolute feature importance; clear error when SHAP is unavailable. |
| LIME explanations | Local `LimeTabularExplainer` explanations; available where the `lime` package imports successfully. |
| Threshold analysis | Candidate-threshold evaluation on validation scores with F1-based recommendation and FP/FN trade-off charts. |
| Reports and exports | Export metrics (JSON), threshold analysis (CSV), and evaluation charts (PNG), including bundled export. |
| Application settings and information | Settings page; About/Limitations page showing version, academic notice, technology stack, and known limitations. |

Features marked optional (SHAP, LIME) degrade gracefully: the application continues without explanations and reports the unavailability explicitly. No unfinished feature is documented as completed.

