## Machine Learning Methodology

The implemented workflow is:

1. **Dataset import** — user selects an AI4I-style CSV via the desktop GUI.
2. **Validation** — schema validation of required columns, target binarity, categories, and numeric domains.
3. **Preprocessing** — imputation, encoding, and scaling fitted on the training partition only; stratified train/test split (80/20, seed 42).
4. **Feature engineering** — `temperature_diff`, `power_kw`, `wear_rate_proxy`, `torque_normalized`, `temp_wear_interaction` derived from the six canonical inputs.
5. **Training** — one of four candidate classifiers is fitted on the training partition; the holdout set is never touched during training.
6. **Evaluation** — classification metrics computed on the untouched held-out test set.
7. **Model selection** — all four candidates compared on identical partitions; best model selected by F1 (configurable).
8. **Prediction** — single-record inference through the saved pipeline with threshold applied to positive-class probability.
9. **Explanation** — SHAP (global + local) or LIME (local) attribution on demand.
10. **Reporting** — metrics, threshold analysis, and charts exported to JSON/CSV/PNG.

### Candidate models

| Model | Purpose at BCA project level |
|-------|------------------------------|
| Logistic Regression | Linear baseline classifier with `class_weight` for imbalance; interpretable coefficients. |
| Random Forest | Ensemble of decision trees with `class_weight`; robust to non-linear patterns. |
| Gradient Boosting | Additive boosting classifier; uses a pure-NumPy fallback (`GradientBoostingFallbackClassifier`) when the scikit-learn native extension is unavailable on Windows. |
| XGBoost | Gradient boosting with `scale_pos_weight` computed from the training partition only. |

No benchmark accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, or ROC-AUC results are claimed. Metrics depend on the user-imported dataset and are computed at runtime.

---

## Explainable Artificial Intelligence

MaintiQ integrates two explainability backends, used on demand and never blocking a prediction:

### SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations)

- **Local explanation:** `shap.KernelExplainer` over the positive-class probability, model-agnostic and compatible with all four estimators including the Gradient Boosting fallback. Produces per-feature contribution values for a single prediction.
- **Global explanation:** mean absolute SHAP values across a bounded background sample, producing ranked feature importance.
- Requires a background reference sample of training rows; cannot produce an explanation without it.

### LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations)

- **Local explanation:** `lime.lime_tabular.LimeTabularExplainer` over the processed feature space using the same preprocessing the fitted model expects.
- Requires a training reference sample; inherently local (no global importance).

### Windows compatibility limitation

SHAP imports `sklearn.cluster` internally, whose native `.pyd` extensions can be blocked by Windows Application Control / Smart App Control policies. In such environments `import shap` fails and MaintiQ reports a clear `ExplanationError` rather than crashing or fabricating explanations. LIME remains available where the `lime` package imports successfully. The application continues to run for import, training, prediction, and reporting without explanations.

Explanations describe model behaviour; they are not causal claims and must not be interpreted as maintenance instructions.

---

## Dataset Information

MaintiQ uses the **AI4I 2020 Predictive Maintenance Dataset** from the UCI Machine Learning Repository:

https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/601/ai4i+2020+predictive+maintenance+dataset

### Supported input variables

| Variable | Source header | Type | Values / domain | Unit | Role |
|----------|--------------|------|-----------------|------|------|
| Machine type | `Type` | Categorical | `L`, `M`, `H` | category | Predictive input |
| Air temperature | `Air temperature [K]` | Numeric | > 0 | Kelvin (K) | Predictive input |
| Process temperature | `Process temperature [K]` | Numeric | > 0 | Kelvin (K) | Predictive input |
| Rotational speed | `Rotational speed [rpm]` | Numeric | > 0 | rpm | Predictive input |
| Torque | `Torque [Nm]` | Numeric | >= 0 | Nm | Predictive input |
| Tool wear | `Tool wear [min]` | Numeric | >= 0 | minutes | Predictive input |

### Target

- **Machine failure** (`Machine failure`) — binary target: `0` = no failure, `1` = failure.

### Important

- The AI4I 2020 dataset is **not bundled** with the application. Users import their own supported CSV dataset through the Dataset Explorer or the File > Import dataset menu.
- No default pre-trained model is supplied. A model must be trained after importing a dataset before prediction is possible.
- No benchmark metrics are claimed without training and evaluation on the real dataset.

Identifier columns (`UDI`, `Product ID`) and target-derived failure-mode indicators (`TWF`, `HDF`, `PWF`, `OSF`, `RNF`) are excluded from predictive input to prevent target leakage.

---

## Architecture

MaintiQ follows a clean, layered architecture with strict separation between the presentation layer and data-science logic.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    User[User] --> GUI[PySide6 Desktop GUI<br/>QMainWindow, 12 pages]
    GUI --> Service[ApplicationService<br/>Orchestration layer]
    
    Service --> Import[ImportWorkflowService<br/>CSV load & validation]
    Service --> Trainer[ModelTrainer<br/>LR / RF / GB / XGB]
    Service --> Registry[ModelRegistry<br/>JSON-backed, SHA-256 verified]
    Service --> Predict[PredictionService<br/>Single-record inference]
    Service --> Report[ReportService<br/>JSON / CSV / PNG export]
    Service --> Eval[EvaluationService<br/>Metrics & threshold analysis]
    Service --> Explain[Explainability<br/>SHAP & LIME]
    
    Import --> Data[Dataset artefacts<br/>%LOCALAPPDATA%/MaintiQ]
    Trainer --> Registry
    Predict --> Registry
    Eval --> Report
    
    GUI -.-> Worker[QRunnable / QThreadPool<br/>Background workers]
    Worker --> Service
```

### Modular architecture

- **Presentation/UI** (`src/maintiq/ui`) — PySide6 `QMainWindow` with 12 thin page widgets. Pages delegate all data, training, prediction, and reporting work to the injected `ApplicationService`; no model-training logic lives in UI modules. Long-running tasks run on `QRunnable`/`QThreadPool` background workers so the UI never freezes.
- **Application services** (`src/maintiq/services`) — `ApplicationService` orchestrates import, training, prediction, explanation, and reporting. Independent of PySide6 and dependency-injectable for testing.
- **ML services** (`src/maintiq/ml`) — preprocessing, feature engineering, training factory, pipeline, registry, evaluation, threshold analysis, comparison, prediction, and explainability.
- **Data** (`src/maintiq/data`) — dataset schema, CSV loader, validator, profiler, and import workflow.
- **Core** (`src/maintiq/core`) — typed configuration, rotating-file logging, custom exceptions, path resolution.

Writable runtime data (datasets, models, registry, logs, settings, reports) is stored under the per-user application-data directory (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\MaintiQ` on Windows), never inside a protected installation directory.

---

## Installation and Usage

### 1. Prerequisites

- Windows 10 or 11
- Python 3.10, 3.11, 3.12, or 3.13
- `pip` and `venv`

### 2. Repository cloning

```powershell
git clone https://github.com/ARJUN-0402/MaintiQ---Software.git
cd MaintiQ---Software
```

### 3. Virtual environment creation

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

### 4. Dependency installation

Runtime dependencies:

```powershell
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Development dependencies (testing, linting):

```powershell
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
```

Packaging dependencies (PyInstaller portable build):

```powershell
pip install -r requirements-packaging.txt
```

### 5. Development installation

Install the package in editable mode so `python -m maintiq` resolves:

```powershell
pip install -e .
```

### 6. Application launch

```powershell
python -m maintiq
```

This launches the native PySide6 desktop GUI. The application is offline-capable after installation; no browser or network is required for normal operation.

### 7. Dataset import

Obtain the AI4I 2020 CSV from the UCI repository (see [Dataset Information](#dataset-information)). In the application, use **File > Import dataset** (`Ctrl+O`) or the Dataset Explorer page to select the CSV. The file is validated against the AI4I schema; the original source file is never modified.

### 8. Model training

Navigate to **Model Training**, select one of the four candidate models (Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost), and start training. Training runs on a background worker with progress reporting and safe cancellation. The pipeline is saved and registered in the local model registry.

### 9. Prediction

Navigate to **Predict Failure**, select a registered model and version, enter the six machine attributes, and run inference. The result shows predicted class, failure score, threshold, and any out-of-distribution warnings.

### 10. Report generation

Navigate to **Reports and Downloads**, select a report type (model evaluation metrics JSON, dataset profile JSON, threshold analysis CSV, evaluation charts PNG, or export bundle), choose an output location, and export.

---

## Windows Executable

A portable Windows executable is built with PyInstaller:

- **Location:** `release\MaintiQ-Portable\MaintiQ.exe`
- **Type:** One-file portable executable (no Python installation required on the target machine)
- **Build script:** `scripts\build_windows.ps1`

### Windows requirements

- Windows 10 or 11
- The portable EXE bundles the Python runtime and all dependencies

### Application data location

Writable runtime data (imported datasets, trained models, registry, logs, settings, reports) is stored under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\MaintiQ`, not inside the executable directory.

### Windows Smart App Control limitation

Unsigned `MaintiQ.exe` builds can be blocked by Windows Smart App Control on locked-down systems. Distribution requires an EV code-signed executable or installer, or an end-user policy bypass. Signing does not unblock blocked third-party `.pyd` files (for example, the `sklearn.cluster` extensions that SHAP depends on).

### Installer

An Inno Setup installer script exists at `packaging\maintiq-installer.iss`. The compiled `MaintiQ-Setup.exe` has **not** been produced yet; run `iscc packaging\maintiq-installer.iss` (with Inno Setup 6+ installed) to generate it. No download link is provided.

